Native TOOM

This directory is a new TempleOS implementation, not a claim of authorship
by Terry A. Davis and not an unmodified release of id Software's engine.

TMDATA.BIN and TmData.HH contain state, actor, weapon, sprite, sound, integer
angle tables, gameplay identifiers and deterministic random data derived from
id Software's DOOM source:

  Copyright (C) 1993-1996 id Software, Inc.
  https://github.com/id-Software/DOOM
  revision a77dfb96cb91780ca334d0d4cfd86957558007e0

The data was converted to a compact native format and patched with Freedoom's
five full-bright firing-frame and two shotgun-flash duration changes on
2026-11-11. It is not the unmodified original. The conversion source is tools/toom/Data.mjs; pinned
source paths and SHA-256 digests are in tools/toom/source/source.json.
That tool retrieves and verifies the corresponding original source, keeping
its notices intact. No original C runtime, platform shim or network code is
linked into the TempleOS implementation.

Map collision, wall sliding, fixed-tic actor motion, aiming/shot queries,
damage/death handling, inventory, hit effects, player and actor projectiles,
weapon
control, fixed-tic player movement/view/power/death, actor sight and blast
damage, moving-plane clipping/crushing, door/floor/lift/ceiling controllers
and activation, teleport movement/effects, switch texture/button state,
sector lighting, damaging floors/secrets/exit sectors, line use/crossing/shot
dispatch, shared thinker scheduling, single-player tic order, actor hearing,
player search, facing, chase movement and melee/missile decisions, surface
animation/scrolling, hit
cross-sections and axis/vertex conventions were re-expressed in
native HolyC from p_map.c, p_maputl.c, p_mobj.c, p_inter.c, p_pspr.c,
p_user.c, p_sight.c, p_floor.c, p_doors.c, p_plats.c, p_ceilng.c, p_telept.c,
the door/neighbor/donut/player-sector/line-event/animation/scrolling portions of
p_spec.c, switch texture/button/use portions of p_switch.c, constants from
p_spec.h, lighting effects in p_lights.c, scheduling in p_tick.c, and the
perception/chase routines, ordinary monster attacks/refire, mancubus spread,
lost-soul charge, pain-elemental spawning/death, revenant launch/tracer
homing, arch-vile corpse selection/resurrection and fire/attack, boss/Keen
death triggers, brain target collection/pain/ending explosions/exit,
death/pain/fall
and footstep actions, explosion wiring and three
double-shotgun sound actions in p_enemy.c
at the same id Software revision. Player rebirth/finish/setup and keyboard
command rules follow g_game.c, p_mobj.c, p_pspr.c and p_setup.c. Weapon-overlay
placement, clipping, light selection, column order and the fifty-step
invisibility pattern follow r_things.c and r_draw.c at that revision. These
render into native owned buffers rather than an original platform driver.
Reference paths and digests are in
tools/toom/source/collision.json.
The native path collector uses exact integer intersections, complete grid
edge/corner traversal and deterministic wall-first ties. Motion splits fast
moves symmetrically, preserves odd fixed-point deltas and slides only the
uncommitted remainder. It is not a demo-synchronization claim.

Integer trigonometry preserves the original tables.c samples, including the
cosine extension. Native fixed division uses defined saturation at signed
limits, and angle slope evaluation uses a wide numerator to avoid legacy
32-bit overflow for distant points.
Arch-vile fire spawns at the target x/y/z, correcting the original repeated-x
spawn coordinate. Forced fire/extra-missile placement refreshes spatial
ownership immediately; stale generation handles cannot name replacement actors.
Brain target storage grows with the actor population instead of the original
fixed 32-pointer array. Its normal thinker ordering and awake sound are retained.

The PC-speaker period table in TmSnd.HC and disabled effect selection in
TmSoundBank.HC are derived from Chocolate Doom's
src/i_pcsound.c, Copyright (C) 2005-2014 Simon Howard, licensed under GPL
version 2 or later. This implementation uses GPL version 2. The pinned
reference is Chocolate Doom 3.1.0, revision
35fb1372d10756ca27eca05665bd8a7cebc71c05; its path and hash are recorded in
tools/toom/source/pcsound.json. The reference is retrieved with its original
notice intact. DP format and period measurements were published by Simon
Howard and Andrew Apted at https://soulsphere.org/apocrypha/pcspkr/ . This is
not a claim that the unreleased original DMX PC-speaker implementation was
available. The native decoder rejects short headers before reading fields
and reads only the declared payload, ignoring any trailing bytes. The sound
bank resolves each effect's own DP name as that PC-speaker driver does; it
does not infer sampled-sound aliases from the original sounds.c links.

